tools/contact_sheet.html
docs/art-review/scratch/

# BROWSER-SUITE SPRITE FIXTURES — never art, never committed.
#
# tools/browser_test.mjs writes generated PNGs to these three paths to test the
# 8-direction grid, the rejected-grid path and a flat FX sprite. They are
# scratch output with a 3-run lifetime, not assets.
#
# They have been committed by accident TWICE, both times by `git add -A` run
# while a suite had left them on disk — once, then removed, then re-added one
# commit later by the same reflex. A gate that checks the tree is clean does
# not stop that: the tree was clean, the fixtures were simply in it. Ignoring
# them is what actually makes the mistake unavailable.
#
# If real art ever lands at these paths it must be force-added deliberately
# (`git add -f`) and this block removed, which is the point — it should take a
# decision, not a habit.
assets/sprites/enemy/skulker.png
assets/sprites/enemy/flit.png
assets/sprites/fx/material.png

# CREDENTIALS — there is no key file in this repo, and these lines exist to
# keep it that way.
#
# PIXELLAB_API_KEY lives in the environment. tools/pixellab.mjs reads
# process.env and passes it to curl through a stdin config (`-K -`), so it is
# never in argv, never in `ps` and never written to disk; the request body goes
# to a temp dir outside the working tree. None of that stops somebody pasting
# the key into a file "just for a minute" and then running `git add -A` — which
# is exactly how the sprite fixtures above got committed twice.
#
# Same principle as that block: ignoring the shape makes the mistake
# UNAVAILABLE rather than merely detectable. tools/secret_gate.mjs checks the
# other half — that no tracked file or staged change carries the key already,
# since .gitignore does nothing about a file that is already tracked.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
secrets.json
pixellab.key
